```console\n41:## Anonymity (double-blind)\n43:- All submissions anonymised, supplementary and linked material and code included; violations are desk rejected (Handbook). Default style mode (\"no option\") prints \"Anonymous Author(s)\", line numbers (do not refer to them), the footer \"Submitted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Do not distribute.\", and hides the `ack` environment. `preprint` shows name\n44:- No acknowledgments, funding statement or institution-revealing URL at submission; the repository link must be anonymous (\"your responsibility to guarantee anonymous browsing\"). Own earlier work is cited in the third person. Aggressive advertising of a paper under submission may be a violation (Handbook).\n45:- A paper written for this delivery should carry no author names, institution or non-anonymous repository URL; code is delivered as an anonymised archive (§Code).\n50:- 16 items: Claims; Limitations; Theory assumptions and proofs; Experimental result reproducibility; Open access to data and code; Experimental setting/details; Experiment statistical significance; Experiments compute resources; Code of ethics; Broader impacts; Safeguards; Licenses for existing assets; New assets; Crowdsourcing and human subjects; IRB approvals; Declaration of LLM usage.\n52:- Checklist items this study must answer from what it did, not from template phrases: statistical significance (error bars, how computed), compute resources (CPU-only, runtime), reproducibility and code/data access (public data with its source and licence; code as supplement), licences of existing assets (the data matrices, the `benchpred` code and the baselines' code), safeguards/ethics (no ne\n54:## LLM and agent use\n56:- Must be described \"in the experimental setup (or equivalent)\" if it \"is an important, original, or non-standard component of the approach\"; spell checking, grammar, editing aid and basic [redacted] assistance need not be documented; agents/LLMs cannot be authors; prompt injection is prohibited (Handbook). Checklist item 16 asks the same; the submission form has a confidential `LLM_usage` multi-sele\n57:- **Flag for `paper-repo`:** this study was planned, run and written by LLM agents. The objective requires truthful reporting and the venue asks for the declaration, so item 16 and the paper must state it plainly (what the agents did; the human role, if any, as the objective says). Whether it belongs in the setup section or the checklist only is a call for `paper-repo` with `internal-review-rep\n59:## Supplementary material and code\n62:- Code and data: one ZIP (`.zip`, `.tgz` or `.gz`), **≤ 100 MB**, anonymised, self-contained and executable if possible, with a specification of dependencies and training/evaluation code (Handbook; OpenReview form `supplementary_material`). Written appendices must not go in the ZIP.\n63:- The objective additionally requires (outside the venue's text): environment and dependency versions, data acquisition steps and data version, seeds, run commands and expected runtime, and a script-to-table/figure map. These belong in the code archive's README and in the paper's reproducibility parts. The task data (≈80 MB) should not be bundled if it pushes the ZIP over 100 MB; the README g\n64:- New datasets/assets: answer the checklist items and anonymise links. None are planned here.\n85:| Anonymous submission, no acknowledgments, anonymous links | Handbook |\n87:| LLM-use description if important/original/non-standard; checklist item 16 | Handbook |\n89:| Code ZIP ≤ 100 MB, anonymised (if code is provided) | OpenReview form |\n```", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "30295dc3-279e-555b-a3ba-acb0fe5e932e", "at": 1795676098.581899, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 20403, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "venue-repo", "actor": "venue-repo", "execution_id": "d50b760bd4386c2bd0eb94b03cfb873f", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "